      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2009 British Association of Plastic, Reconstructive and Aesthetic Surgeons. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "This study was performed to determine if pretreatment application of a topical anesthetic skin refrigerant reduced discomfort during botulinum toxin injection."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Twenty patients were assigned to four groups determined by side of the face pretreated with skin refrigerant and side receiving the first injection. On a Visual Analog Scale of 0-10 patients rated discomfort levels after injections in the glabellar complex with and without pretreatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Mean discomfort rating for the pretreated side was 3.1, while the mean discomfort rating for the non-pretreated side was 4.5. Discomfort was not affected by the side sprayed (p=0.33) nor by administering the injection to the sprayed side first (p=0.37). The paired t-test revealed a significant difference between discomfort levels on the pretreated and non-pretreated sides (p=0.038) yielding a 95% confidence interval of (-2.71, -0.09)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Topical anesthetic skin refrigerant significantly reduces discomfort in a cost-effective manner for reported by patients undergoing botulinum injections."
          }
        ]
      },
